::PC_U01_L06_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Evaluate 3
{
~A. 12#This multiplies the base by the exponent.
~B. 64#This is 4, not 3
=C. 81#Correct. 3 = 3 times 3 times 3 times 3 = 81.
~D. 7#This adds the base and exponent.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is log base 2 of 16
{
~A. 8#2⁸ is much larger than 16.
~B. 14#This subtracts values rather than finding the exponent.
~C. 2#2² = 4, not 16. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
=D. 4#Correct. 2⁴ = 16. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which model represents 12 percent growth from an initial value of 200
{
~A. y \= 200(0.88)t#This represents 12 percent decay.
~C. y \= 200(0.12)t#This uses 0.12 as the whole factor, not a 12 percent increase factor.
=B. y \= 200(1.12)t#Correct. A 12 percent increase uses growth factor 1.12.
~D. y \= 12(200)t#This switches the initial value and growth structure.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Rewrite 4 = 64 in logarithmic form.
{
~B. log base 3 of 64 \= 4#This switches the base and exponent.
~C. log base 64 of 4 \= 3#This uses the output as the base.
~D. log base 4 of 3 \= 64#This places the exponent as the input.
=A. log base 4 of 64 \= 3#Correct. The logarithm asks what exponent on 4 gives 64.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
In y = 80(0.75)t, what does 0.75 indicate
{
~B. 75 percent growth#A factor less than 1 indicates decay, not growth.
=A. 25 percent decay#Correct. Multiplying by 0.75 keeps 75 percent, so the amount decreases by 25 percent.
~C. initial value 0.75#The initial value is 80.
~D. time period 0.75#The variable t represents the number of periods.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q06::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is the domain of f(x) = log base 10 of x²
{
~A. x >\= 0#The input 0 is not allowed.
=D. x > 0#Correct. A logarithmic input must be greater than 0.
~B. x < 0#Negative inputs are not allowed for this real logarithm.
~C. all real numbers#Logarithmic inputs must be positive.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q07::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Solve 2⁵ = 32.
{
~B. x \= 30#This subtracts values instead of matching powers.
~C. x \= 4#2⁴ = 16, not 32. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
=A. x \= 5#Correct. 2⁵ = 32. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
~D. x \= 16#This confuses the output with an exponent.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q08::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is log base 5 of 1
{
~A. 1#5 = 5, not 1. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
=C. 0#Correct. Any valid base raised to 0 equals 1.
~B. 5#5 is not 1. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
~D. -1#5 = 1/5, not 1.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q09::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which factor represents 6 percent decay
{
~B. 1.06#This represents 6 percent growth.
~C. 0.06#This keeps only 6 percent, not a 6 percent decrease.
~D. 6#This is not a percent-change factor.
=A. 0.94#Correct. A 6 percent decrease leaves 94 percent, or 0.94.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q10::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Which exponential equation matches log base 7 of 49 = 2
{
~A. 49 \= 2#This reverses the relationship incorrectly.
=C. 7 \= 49#Correct. The base 7 raised to exponent 2 gives 49.
~B. 7 + 2 \= 49#Logarithms are connected to exponents, not addition.
~D. 2² \= 49#This switches the exponent and base.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q11::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
For y = 40(2)t, what is the initial value
{
~B. 80#This is the value after one doubling period, not the initial value.
=A. 40#Correct. When t = 0, the model starts at 40.
~C. 2#The 2 is the growth factor.
~D. t#The variable t represents time or number of periods.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q12::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is log base 10 of 1000
{
~A. 1#10 = 10, not 1000. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
~B. 2#10 = 100, not 1000. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
=C. 3#Correct. 10 = 1000. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
~D. 100#This confuses the output with an exponent.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q13::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which model represents repeated doubling from an initial value of 15
{
~A. y \= 2(15)t#This switches the initial value and factor.
=D. y \= 15(2)t#Correct. The initial value is 15 and the factor 2 doubles each period.
~B. y \= 15 + 2t#This is linear growth, not exponential doubling.
~C. y \= 15(0.5)t#This represents halving, not doubling.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q14::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What input restriction applies to f(x) = log base 3 of (x - 4)
{
~A. x \= 4#At x = 4, the input is 0, which is not allowed.
~B. all real numbers#The logarithmic expression has a domain restriction.
~D. x < 4#This makes x - 4 negative, which is not allowed.
=C. x > 4#Correct. The logarithmic input x - 4 must be positive.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q15::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
If a population is multiplied by 1.03 each year, what is the annual percent change
{
~A. 0.03 percent growth#The decimal 0.03 corresponds to 3 percent, not 0.03 percent.
=C. 3 percent growth#Correct. A factor of 1.03 represents a 3 percent increase.
~B. 3 percent decay#A factor greater than 1 indicates growth.
~D. 103 percent decay#The factor 1.03 means the total is 103 percent of the previous amount, not a 103 percent decrease.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q16::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Which statement is equivalent to log base 2 of 8 = 3
{
~A. 3 \= 8#This switches base and exponent.
=B. 2³ \= 8#Correct. The logarithm means 2 raised to 3 equals 8.
~C. 2³ \= 3#This uses the input as the exponent.
~D. 8 \= 2#This reverses the equation. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q17::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which model represents 20 percent decay from an initial value of 600
{
=D. y \= 600(0.80)t#Correct. A 20 percent decrease leaves 80 percent each period.
~A. y \= 600(1.20)t#This represents 20 percent growth.
~B. y \= 20(600)t#This does not use the initial value and decay factor correctly.
~C. y \= 600 - 20t#This is linear decrease, not exponential decay.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q18::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
What is log base 4 of 16
{
=A. 2#Correct. 4 = 16. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
~B. 4#4 is 256, not 16.
~C. 12#This subtracts values instead of finding the exponent.
~D. 1#4 = 4, not 16. Review the lesson rule to identify why this choice does not match the required step.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q19::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
What horizontal asymptote does the basic model y = 3 approach
{
~A. x \= 3#This uses the base as a vertical line.
=D. y \= 0#Correct. Basic exponential functions with no vertical shift approach y = 0.
~B. x \= 0#A horizontal asymptote is written as y equals a value.
~C. y \= 3#The base is 3, but the graph approaches 0 as x decreases.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q20::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Why must the input of a logarithm be positive
{
~A. Because logarithms always produce positive outputs.#Logarithm outputs can be negative, zero, or positive.
~C. Because the base must be negative.#Logarithm bases used here are positive and not equal to 1.
~D. Because logarithms are linear functions.#Logarithms are not linear functions.
=B. Because exponential outputs are positive.#Correct. A logarithm reverses an exponential relationship, and exponential outputs are positive.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q21::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which first step best supports a solution for Exponential and Logarithmic Models
{
~A. Skip directly to an answer without naming the given information.#Skipping the setup often leads to using the wrong method.
~C. Use any formula from a different unit.#A different unit may use different assumptions or tools.
=B. Identify the given representation, quantities, and restrictions before calculating.#Correct. A strong Precalculus solution begins by identifying the representation and restrictions so the method matches the task.
~D. Base the answer only on how a graph or expression appears.#Appearance can be misleading unless it is supported by values or relationships.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q22::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
What evidence should a student use when explaining Exponential and Logarithmic Models
{
~A. Ignore labels and units because they are not part of the calculation.#Labels and units help determine what the quantities mean.
=B. Use labeled values, equations, graphs, or tables from the problem as evidence.#Correct. Evidence must come from the given mathematical representation, not from a guess or unrelated rule.
~C. Use a future topic even when the current lesson gives enough information.#The current lesson evidence should be used before reaching for later topics.
~D. Use the longest answer choice as evidence.#Answer length is not mathematical evidence.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q23::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which response best shows mastery of Exponential and Logarithmic Models
{
~A. Change the given values to make the computation easier.#Changing given values changes the problem.
~C. Give only a final number with no reasoning.#A final number alone does not show how the relationship was used.
~D. Copy vocabulary without applying it to the problem.#Vocabulary must be connected to the actual representation or calculation.
=B. Show the steps, connect them to the lesson relationship, and interpret the result.#Correct. Mastery requires both accurate work and an interpretation connected to the lesson relationship.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q24::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
What should be checked before finalizing work involving Exponential and Logarithmic Models
{
~A. Check only that the answer choice is short.#Length does not show correctness.
=D. Check that notation, units, domain restrictions, and the final interpretation match the task.#Correct. These checks help confirm that the answer is mathematically valid for the stated task.
~B. Remove restrictions because they make the problem harder.#Restrictions are part of the mathematical meaning.
~C. Assume every representation has the same domain.#Different representations may show different restrictions.
}

::PC_U01_L06_Q25::
Question ID: PC_U01_L06_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
Which error should be avoided when working with Exponential and Logarithmic Models
{
~A. Writing the given information first.#Writing givens first is a helpful setup, not the error.
=C. Using an unrelated formula or visual pattern without checking the lesson conditions.#Correct. Appearance alone is not reliable; the method must match the conditions in the lesson.
~B. Explaining why a theorem, identity, or model applies.#Explaining why a method applies strengthens the solution.
~D. Checking the final answer in the context of the problem.#Checking in context is a good habit, not an error.}
